(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-candidate"]={"plain":{"bills":{"candidate":{"slug":"candidate","title":"The Candidate Integrity Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-0-1","heading":"\u00a7 0-1. Short Title","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, AND DEFINITIONS","body":"This Act may be cited as the Candidate Integrity Act.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, AND DEFINITIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-0-2","heading":"\u00a7 0-2. Findings","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, AND DEFINITIONS","body":"Congress finds the following:\n\n1. Funds raised for a Federal candidacy are contributed for a public purpose \u2014 to seek office \u2014 and their diversion to the personal enrichment of the candidate or the candidate's family is a corruption of that purpose and creates the appearance of corruption.\n\n2. When a candidate directs contributed funds to entities the candidate or the candidate's family owns, or draws compensation and benefits from a campaign beyond the replacement of forgone earnings, the candidacy becomes a vehicle for private gain, and a contributor's payment to the candidate's own business is indistinguishable in effect from a payment to the candidate.\n\n3. Existing law permits a candidate to draw a salary from the candidate's own campaign, so that a person of ordinary means may seek office without personal ruin. This Act preserves that salary and confines it, so that the provision serves the person who needs it and not the person who does not.\n\n4. A payment of value to a candidate or the candidate's family, from any organization organized or operating to influence Federal elections, presents a risk of quid pro quo corruption and its appearance regardless of the tax or regulatory character of the organization, and regardless of whether the organization's expenditures are independent.\n\n5. The interest served by this Act is the prevention of quid pro quo corruption and its appearance. This Act does not rest on, and its findings do not invoke, any interest in equalizing influence or resources among candidates. This Act regulates the flow of value to candidates and their families, and does not limit the speech, the independent expenditures, or the contributions received of any organization.\n\n6. A candidate who holds a financial position whose value turns on the outcome of the candidate's own election holds an interest adverse to the electorate's \u2014 including, where the position pays on defeat, a financial interest in losing \u2014 and the integrity of Federal elections is injured by the existence of that interest whether or not any act is affected. The same adversity attends such a position held by a person who, for compensation, conducts or advises the candidacy, whose knowledge of the campaign and capacity to affect it are an insider's. The interest served by \u00a7 1-10 is the integrity of Federal elections and the elimination of that adversity. It is not an interest in limiting any contribution, expenditure, or speech, and \u00a7 1-10 limits none.\n\n7. A communication about a candidate is understood by the person who receives it to express the view of the person who published it. Where the publisher was paid to publish it and the payment is not disclosed, the audience is deprived of the one fact it would need in order to weigh the communication \u2014 that the communication was bought, and by whom \u2014 and paid advertising reaches the audience in the form of independent opinion. The electorate's interest in knowing who paid for a communication about a candidate does not vary with the medium through which the communication is published.\n\n8. That interest is served today for a mailing, a broadcast advertisement, and an advertisement placed for a fee on a digital platform, and is not served where the same money buys the same message from a person who publishes it to that person's own audience. The definition of general public political advertising in force excludes communications over the internet except where a fee is paid to a website, application, or advertising platform to place or promote them. A payment made to the speaker rather than to the platform therefore carries no disclaimer, and the Federal Election Commission declined in its most recent rulemaking on internet communications to reach such a payment.\n\n9. A payment routed through an intermediary, or reported under a description that does not disclose that a communication was purchased, defeats disclosure as completely as a payment never reported. Disclosure of a purchased communication must follow the payment through each person who transmits it, and must identify the communication that was purchased.\n\n10. The interest served by \u00a7 2-1B is the interest of the audience for a communication in knowing that the communication was paid for and by whom. It is not an interest in the content of any communication, in the amount any person may spend or receive, or in equalizing influence or resources among speakers, and \u00a7 2-1B limits no communication and no amount.\n\n11. A communication about a candidate published through an account that a political committee or covered political organization operates under a name that does not identify it reaches the audience as the view of the person the account purports to be \u2014 a neighbor, a local publication, an independent creator \u2014 and the audience is deprived of the same fact as where a publisher is paid: who is speaking. The interest served by \u00a7 2-1C is the interest of the audience in knowing that the speaker is an organization organized or operating to influence Federal elections, and which one. The same fact is withheld where an account that presents itself as independent is funded by such an organization. It is not an interest in the content of any communication, and \u00a7 2-1C limits no communication and no account."},{"anchor":"sec-0-3","heading":"\u00a7 0-3. Definitions","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, AND DEFINITIONS","body":"For purposes of this Act:\n\n**(a) \"Candidate\"** has the meaning given in 52 U.S.C. \u00a7 30101(2), and includes an individual who has taken any action to seek nomination or election to Federal office, whether or not that individual is or becomes a covered person under the Executive Integrity Act or the Congressional Integrity Act.\n\n**(b) \"Political committee\"** means any authorized committee of a candidate, any leadership political action committee, any joint fundraising committee, any political party committee, and any separate segregated fund or nonconnected committee; and, for purposes of Sections 1 and 2, also includes an inaugural committee, a legal expense fund or legal defense fund, and a Presidential library foundation.\n\n**(c) \"Covered political organization\"** means, in addition to a political committee, any organization that raises or spends funds for the purpose of influencing a Federal election, or that in a calendar year devotes more than 25 percent of its total expenditures to that purpose, without regard to its form of organization or its treatment under the Internal Revenue Code \u2014 including an independent-expenditure-only committee, an organization described in section 527, and an organization described in section 501(c)(4), 501(c)(5), or 501(c)(6).\n\n**(c-2) What this Act reaches of such an organization.** This Act reaches a covered political organization that is not a political committee only as to: a payment it makes to, or a transaction it enters with, a candidate, a covered person, or a family member; the assembly of funds disclosed under \u00a7 2-1A; compensation it provides to which \u00a7 2-1B applies; and an account it operates to which \u00a7 2-1C applies. It reaches no other activity of the organization.\n\n**(d) \"Family member\"** means the spouse or domestic partner of a candidate, and the parent, child, sibling, grandparent, grandchild, or dependent of a candidate, together with the relations enumerated in 5 U.S.C. \u00a7 3110(a)(3). Domestic partner has the meaning given in the Executive Integrity Act \u00a7 0-3(g).\n\n**(e) \"Beneficial interest\"** means a direct or indirect ownership, equity, profits, or economic interest, whether held directly or through any entity, trust, nominee, or intermediary, and includes an interest attributed under \u00a7 1-4.\n\n**(f) \"Covered person\"** means a covered person under the Executive Integrity Act or the Congressional Integrity Act.\n\n**(g) \"The Office\"** means the Federal Election Commission.\n\n**(g-1) The Commission's role confined.** The Commission receives filings, screens complaints, publishes, and refers. It shall not determine or impose any financial remedy. Every disgorgement, penalty, and other monetary consequence under this Act is determined by a court of the United States.\n\n**(h) \"Communication\"** means a message published or transmitted to the public or to any segment of the public, by any means and through any medium, whether or not that medium existed on the date of enactment of this Act, including a post, image, video, livestream, broadcast, audio program, newsletter, and a message published to the subscribers to or followers of an account, channel, or feed.\n\n**(i) \"Compensation\"** means anything of value given, promised, or forgiven, whether or not in money, whether provided before or after publication, whether fixed or contingent upon any measure of audience, engagement, or outcome, and whether provided to the person who publishes or, at that person's direction, to another. Compensation includes a fee, a salary, a retainer, a commission, a bonus, a share of revenue, a payment for advice, strategy, consulting, surrogacy, appearance, content, production, or media, the provision of goods, services, staff, travel, lodging, or event access, and the forgiveness of an obligation. Compensation does not include a thing of value made available to the general public on the same terms, and does not include merchandise, event access, or a similar thing of value made available on the same terms to the supporters, members, volunteers, or contributors of a payer generally and not in exchange for, on the condition of, or in the expectation of the publication of a communication. The value of compensation not provided in money is its fair market value at the time it is provided.\n\n**(j) \"Covered payer\"** means a candidate, a political committee, and a covered political organization.\n\n**(k) \"Promoter\"** means a person who receives compensation to which \u00a7 2-1B applies. A promoter may be a natural person or an entity, and may publish under the promoter's own name, under a pseudonym, through an account, channel, or feed the promoter controls, or through another person.\n\n**(l) \"Intermediary\"** means a person who transmits compensation from one person to another, or who arranges, procures, or manages the publication of a communication for compensation, including an agency, a vendor, a talent or creator network, a marketing service, and a person who contracts with a promoter on another person's behalf.\n\n**(m) \"Platform\"** means a person who provides a service through which other persons publish communications.\n\n**(n) \"Policy position\"** means a position on a matter of public policy.\n\n**(n-1) \"Policy position on a pending decision\"** means a policy position on a measure pending or proposed in a legislature, on a regulatory action pending or proposed, on a nomination or appointment pending or proposed, or on a ballot measure. Every policy position on a pending decision is a policy position.\n\n**(o) \"Account\"** means an account, channel, page, profile, feed, website, or equivalent through which a person publishes communications, whether provided by a platform or maintained by the person.\n\n**(p) \"Operate,\"** with respect to an account, means to control, alone or with others, what is published through it, whether directly or through an officer, employee, agent, contractor, or other person acting at the direction of the person who controls it, and without regard to the name in which the account is held or registered. A person that owns a majority of, or otherwise controls, the person that operates an account operates it."},{"anchor":"sec-1-1","heading":"\u00a7 1-1. General Rule","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 CANDIDATE SELF-ENRICHMENT","body":"A candidate shall not derive personal financial benefit from the candidate's pursuit of Federal office, or from any political committee, except:\n\n**(a)** a salary under \u00a7 1-2;\n\n**(b)** reimbursement of documented, bona fide expenses actually incurred in the pursuit of office, at cost and without markup; and\n\n**(c)** the ordinary, incidental benefit of goods and services purchased by a political committee at arm's length from an unrelated third party for a bona fide campaign purpose.\n\nAny other flow of value from a political committee to a candidate or a family member, whether direct or indirect and regardless of form, title, or characterization, is prohibited.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 1 \u2014 CANDIDATE SELF-ENRICHMENT","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-1-2","heading":"\u00a7 1-2. The Candidate Salary \u2014 Preserved and Confined","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 CANDIDATE SELF-ENRICHMENT","body":"**(a) Entitlement.** A candidate may draw a salary from the candidate's own authorized committee. This salary exists so that a person of ordinary means may seek Federal office without forgoing the income needed to live, and it shall be construed to serve that purpose.\n\n**(b) Cap.** The salary shall not exceed the annual salary of the Federal office the candidate seeks, prorated for the portion of the year during which the candidacy continues.\n\n**(b-1) No prior-income limitation.** The salary is not limited by the candidate's prior income.\n\n**(c) Period.** The salary may be drawn only during the candidacy. It ends upon the candidate's election, the end of the candidacy, or the candidate's assumption of any Federal office, whichever occurs first.\n\n**(d) A sitting covered person draws no salary.** A candidate who is a sitting covered person, or who otherwise receives a salary from the United States Government, shall draw no salary from any political committee. Compensation of a serving official by a political committee is governed by \u00a7 1-5.\n\n**(e) One flow only.** The salary under this Section is the sole permissible flow of personal compensation from a political committee to a candidate. No reimbursement beyond \u00a7 1-1(b), no rent, no royalty, no licensing fee, no consulting or advisory fee, and no other payment may be made from a political committee to a candidate."},{"anchor":"sec-1-3","heading":"\u00a7 1-3. Prohibited Self-Dealing","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 CANDIDATE SELF-ENRICHMENT","body":"**(a) Payments to candidate-owned entities.** No political committee or covered political organization shall make any payment to an entity in which a candidate, a covered person, or a family member holds a beneficial interest. This prohibition is not satisfied by a showing that the payment was at fair market value.\n\n**(b) Rent, venues, and property.** No political committee or covered political organization shall pay for the use of real or personal property owned, in whole or in part, by a candidate, a covered person, or a family member.\n\n**(b-1) Uncompensated use permitted.** Nothing in this Section prohibits the use of property owned by a candidate, a covered person, or a family member without charge. Such use is an in-kind contribution, is reported as one, and is subject to the limits and disclosure applicable to contributions.\n\n**(c) Intellectual property licensed royalty-free.** Any name, likeness, image, brand, trademark, or other intellectual property of a candidate, a covered person, or a family member that is used by a political committee or covered political organization is licensed to that committee or organization royalty-free. All revenue from merchandise or other goods bearing such property accrues to the committee or organization. No portion of such revenue may flow to the candidate, the covered person, or a family member.\n\n**(d) Family payroll and contracts.** A payment from a political committee or covered political organization to a family member, whether as salary, wages, consulting or advisory fee, commission, or vendor contract, is permitted only if the work is bona fide, the compensation does not exceed the fair market value of comparable services rendered by an unrelated person, the arrangement is certified by an independent person, and the arrangement is publicly disclosed under \u00a7 2-1. A payment not meeting each of these conditions is prohibited.\n\n**(d-1) Certification.** The certification required by subsection (d) is a written statement, made under penalty of perjury by a person who is not the candidate, the covered person, or a family member, stating the basis for the compensation and that it does not exceed fair market value. The statement may rely on the comparables schedule published under subsection (d-2) in lieu of an independent appraisal, in which case the committee's own treasurer or compliance officer may serve as the certifying person. Nothing in this subsection precludes certification by a paid independent appraiser instead.\n\n**(d-2) Comparables schedule.** The Commission shall publish, and periodically update, a schedule of reasonable compensation ranges for common categories of campaign work, for use under subsection (d-1). Reliance on the published schedule does not itself establish compliance where the work actually performed does not match the category claimed."},{"anchor":"sec-1-4","heading":"\u00a7 1-4. Attribution","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 CANDIDATE SELF-ENRICHMENT","body":"**(a)** A beneficial interest held by a family member is attributed to the candidate for purposes of \u00a7\u00a7 1-1 and 1-3.\n\n**(b)** A beneficial interest held by any other person or entity is attributed to the candidate where the candidate or a family member funded it, controls it, directs its use, or derives economic benefit from it, on the standard of attribution under the Executive Integrity Act \u00a7 3-2.\n\n**(c)** Form, title, characterization, the independence of any intermediary, and the interposition of any entity or trust are not determinative under this Section."},{"anchor":"sec-1-4a","heading":"\u00a7 1-4A. Attribution Enforced Civilly Only","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 CANDIDATE SELF-ENRICHMENT","body":"**(a)** The attribution standard of \u00a7 1-4 is enforced civilly only. No criminal liability under \u00a7 2-3 arises from the application of that standard, from any assessment of fair market value, or from any judgment as to whether a person controls, directs, or derives benefit from an interest. Criminal liability attaches only to the discrete acts enumerated in \u00a7 2-3(a), each provable without resort to an elastic standard.\n\n**(b) Concealment defined.** For purposes of this Act, to conceal is to fail to make a disclosure required by Section 2, before the matter is identified by another, where the person knew of the matter and of the obligation to disclose it, or was aware of facts from which that obligation was apparent and did not inquire. A failure to disclose is not concealment where the person did not know and could not reasonably have known of the matter; where the person reasonably relied on a report of a custodian, treasurer, or adviser after disclosing to that person all facts material to the question; or where the person relied on a ruling under \u00a7 2-4 obtained on a complete and accurate description. Reliance is not reasonable where a material fact was withheld or where the person knew or had reason to know the report or ruling was incorrect. The Government bears the burden of establishing concealment.\n\n**(c) Identified by another.** A matter is identified by another on the earliest of: written notice to the person from the Office; the filing of an action under \u00a7 2-2 or \u00a7 2-2A identifying the matter; and publication of the matter in a generally circulated publication or public record. No other event constitutes identification."},{"anchor":"sec-1-5","heading":"\u00a7 1-5. Compensation of Serving Officials","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 CANDIDATE SELF-ENRICHMENT","body":"**(a) Prohibited.** No covered person, and no other officer or employee of the United States above a threshold set in the schedule to this Act, shall, while holding office, receive from a political committee or covered political organization: a salary, retainer, consulting fee, advisory fee, commission, or other compensation for services; or any compensation for services related to the person's official duties or to the electoral activity of the committee or organization.\n\n**(b) Not prohibited.** Subsection (a) does not reach an arm's-length payment unrelated to the person's office and unrelated to the electoral activity of the payer, including a royalty on a work of authorship on terms available to the public, or compensation for teaching at an accredited educational institution. A payment under this subsection is disclosed under \u00a7 2-1."},{"anchor":"sec-1-6","heading":"\u00a7 1-6. Preservation of Speech and Independent Expenditure","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 CANDIDATE SELF-ENRICHMENT","body":"Nothing in this Act limits the independent expenditures, the contributions received, the disbursements for speech, or any other activity of a political committee or covered political organization, except the flow of value to a candidate, a covered person, or a family member prohibited by this Act. This Act imposes no limit on the amount any organization may raise or spend, and shall not be construed to limit the speech of any organization."},{"anchor":"sec-1-7","heading":"\u00a7 1-7. Disclosure of a Prohibited Payment and Related Expenditure","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 CANDIDATE SELF-ENRICHMENT","body":"**(a)** Where a covered political organization makes a payment prohibited by \u00a7 1-3 to a candidate, a covered person, a family member, or an entity in which any of them holds a beneficial interest, the organization and the candidate shall each disclose, within 48 hours and in structured, machine-readable form: the payment, its amount and date; the recipient and the natural persons who control or beneficially own the recipient; and the organization's disbursements supporting or opposing that candidate in the same election cycle.\n\n**(b) Disclosure only.** This Section imposes a disclosure obligation. It creates no presumption, shifts no burden, does not characterize any disbursement as coordinated, does not convert any disbursement into a contribution, and imposes no consequence on any disbursement. The payment itself remains prohibited by \u00a7 1-3 and is remedied under Section 2."},{"anchor":"sec-1-8","heading":"\u00a7 1-8. Leadership Political Action Committees","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 CANDIDATE SELF-ENRICHMENT","body":"**(a)** For the avoidance of doubt, and without limiting the definition of political committee in \u00a7 0-3(b) or implying that any entity within that definition is not otherwise covered, the personal-use prohibition of 52 U.S.C. \u00a7 30114(b), and the self-dealing prohibitions of \u00a7 1-3, apply to a leadership political action committee to the same extent as to an authorized committee.\n\n**(b)** No leadership political action committee shall pay any personal expense of a candidate, a covered person, or a family member, or make any payment to an entity in which any of them holds a beneficial interest."},{"anchor":"sec-1-9","heading":"\u00a7 1-9. Legal Expense Funds, Inaugural Committees, and Library Foundations","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 CANDIDATE SELF-ENRICHMENT","body":"**(a)** For the avoidance of doubt, and without limiting the definition of political committee in \u00a7 0-3(b) or implying that any entity within that definition is not otherwise covered, a legal expense or legal defense fund, an inaugural committee, and a Presidential library foundation are subject to the self-dealing prohibitions of \u00a7 1-3 and the disclosure requirements of \u00a7 2-1 to the same extent as an authorized committee.\n\n**(b)** A legal expense fund may pay only the reasonable, documented fees and costs of legal representation, billed at market rates by counsel not affiliated with the candidate or a family member, and paid directly to counsel. No such fund shall pay a candidate, a covered person, or a family member, or reimburse any of them.\n\n**(c)** Each such fund, committee, and foundation shall disclose each contributor and each disbursement, in structured, machine-readable form, on the schedule applicable to an authorized committee under \u00a7 2-1."},{"anchor":"sec-1-10","heading":"\u00a7 1-10. Positions on the Candidate's Own Election","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 CANDIDATE SELF-ENRICHMENT","body":"**(a) Prohibition.** A candidate shall not enter into, acquire, hold, or maintain a covered event position on the outcome, conduct, timing, or any other aspect of any Federal election in which the person is a candidate, including a contest for nomination, whether the position would gain from the candidate's success or from the candidate's defeat, whether directly or through any structure, arrangement, entity, trust, nominee, or intermediary, and regardless of form, title, or characterization.\n\n**(b) Covered event position.** \"Covered event position\" has the meaning given in the Executive Integrity Act \u00a7 3-3B(c), subject to the exclusions of \u00a7 3-3B(d) of that Act other than the exclusion for State-licensed wagering, which does not apply to a position on an election.\n\n**(c) Attribution.** A position held by a family member is attributed to the candidate, and a position held by any other person is attributed on the standard of \u00a7 1-4(b). A position entered into by another person at the candidate's direction or request, funded by the candidate, or in whose proceeds the candidate is entitled to share, is the candidate's.\n\n**(d) Closure.** A position described in subsection (a) held on the date a person becomes a candidate shall be closed or divested within 30 days of that date.\n\n**(e) Disclosure of other event positions.** A candidate shall disclose, on the statement of candidacy and within 48 hours of any later acquisition, each covered event position the candidate holds on any election, or on the action or inaction of a government or an officer of a government, that is not prohibited by subsection (a). The Office shall publish each disclosure under \u00a7 2-1(c). Disclosure under this subsection is a duty within \u00a7 3-1A(a).\n\n**(f) Void; knowing acceptance barred.** A position entered into in violation of subsection (a) is void, and no venue or counterparty acquires any right upon it. A venue described in the Executive Integrity Act \u00a7 3-3B(j) shall not knowingly accept from a person a covered event position on an election in which that person is a candidate; the statements of candidacy published by the Office are notice of candidacy for purposes of this subsection.\n\n**(g) Remedy.** For purposes of \u00a7 2-2, the value that flowed to the candidate from a violation of this Section is the amount staked upon or paid to acquire the position together with any gain realized upon it. An amount disgorged by reason of this Section is not deductible for any tax purpose, and no such amount is treated as restitution, remediation, or an amount paid to come into compliance with law for purposes of section 162(f)(2) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986.\n\n**(h) No qualification for office.** No consequence under this Section excludes any person from the ballot, from candidacy, or from any office, adds any qualification for any office, or conditions access to the ballot. The consequences of a violation are those of \u00a7 2-2 and \u00a7 2-3 and no others.\n\n**(i) Committee personnel.** Subsection (a) applies, during the period of service or engagement, to each officer and employee of an authorized committee of the candidate, and to each person retained by such a committee for compensation \u2014 whether the compensation is paid or promised, current or deferred \u2014 to provide consulting, advisory, management, communications, fundraising, or other personal services, as it applies to the candidate, with respect to each Federal election in which the candidate is a candidate. A position held on the date the person's service or engagement begins shall be closed or divested within 30 days of that date. Subsection (c) applies with respect to such a person as it applies with respect to the candidate, and subsections (f) and (g) apply to a position prohibited by this subsection, except that nothing in subsection (f) requires a venue to determine whether a person serves or is retained by a committee. \u00a7 2-2 applies to a violation of this subsection, and the value that flowed is measured under subsection (g) and is payable personally by the violator and not from any committee. This subsection reaches no volunteer serving without compensation, and its application is severable from the application of subsection (a) to the candidate."},{"anchor":"sec-2-1","heading":"\u00a7 2-1. Disclosure","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 DISCLOSURE AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a)** Each political committee and covered political organization shall disclose, in structured, machine-readable form and within 48 hours: each payment to any entity in which a candidate, covered person, or family member holds a beneficial interest that is claimed to be permitted; each salary drawn under \u00a7 1-2, with the basis for the cap; and each family-member arrangement under \u00a7 1-3(d), with the certification required by that subsection.\n\n**(b)** The salary drawn under \u00a7 1-2 is public. No candidate is required to disclose income earned before the candidacy.\n\n**(c)** The Office shall publish each disclosure under this Section in a public, searchable, machine-readable, bulk-downloadable database.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 2 \u2014 DISCLOSURE AND ENFORCEMENT","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-2-1a","heading":"\u00a7 2-1A. Bundler Disclosure","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 DISCLOSURE AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a)** Each political committee and each covered political organization shall disclose, in structured, machine-readable form, the identity of each person who forwards, solicits, or is credited by the committee or organization with raising contributions or receipts aggregating more than a threshold set in the schedule to this Act in an election cycle, together with the aggregate amount so raised and credited.\n\n**(a-1) Aggregation reached wherever it occurs.** This Section reaches the assembly of funds without regard to the form of the entity that receives them or the mechanism by which they are assembled, including a conduit, a joint fundraising arrangement, an online contribution platform, and a covered political organization.\n\n**(a-2) Disclosure anchored to conduct, not to the committee's record.** A person is disclosable under subsection (a) where the person transmitted, or arranged for the transmission of, contributions or receipts aggregating more than the threshold, whether or not the committee or organization credited them to that person. Each political committee and covered political organization shall keep records sufficient to identify each such person. A committee that keeps no record of crediting does not thereby escape this Section.\n\n**(a-3) The party who holds the attribution transmits it.** A conduit, a joint fundraising arrangement, and an online contribution platform transmitting contributions or receipts to a political committee or covered political organization shall transmit with them the information it holds identifying any person to whom they are attributable under subsection (a-2). This subsection requires the transmission of information the transmitting party already holds and requires no new collection. It supplies the committee the fact its duty under subsection (a-2) runs on; it does not diminish that duty.\n\n**(b)** The threshold shall be set at a level meaningful enough that disclosure serves the public's interest in knowing who assembles the funds a candidate depends upon, and high enough that ordinary volunteers and small-dollar solicitation are not burdened.\n\n**(c)** This Section requires disclosure of the bundler's identity and the amount raised. It does not limit the amount any person may contribute, solicit, or bundle.\n\n**(d)** The interest served by this Section is the public's interest in knowing who assembles the funds on which a candidate depends, which is the informational interest sustained in Buckley v. Valeo and Citizens United v. FEC. This Section is limited to that interest and reaches no other information.\n\n**(e) Exemption for threat of harassment.** The Office shall exempt a person from disclosure under this Section on a showing of a reasonable probability that disclosure would subject the person or the person's family to threats, harassment, or reprisal. This exemption applies the rule of NAACP v. Alabama and Brown v. Socialist Workers '74 Campaign Committee.\n\n**(e-1) The obligation is suspended while the application is pending.** The disclosure obligation under this Section is suspended as to a person from the filing of an application under subsection (e) until the Office decides it. The Office shall grant or deny an application within the period set in the schedule to this Act.\n\n**(e-2) Content, reasons, and the default.** An application under subsection (e) shall state the facts relied upon. The Office shall state the reasons for a grant or denial. An application neither granted nor denied within the period set in the schedule is granted by operation of law. A grant by operation of law is provisional: it continues until the Office decides the application, and where the Office thereafter denies it on the standard in subsection (e), the disclosure obligation resumes prospectively and the person shall comply within 30 days of the denial. A provisional grant excuses no disclosure withheld after a denial and is not a determination that the showing in subsection (e) was made.\n\n**(e-3) The counterweight.** The Office shall publish annually the number of applications under subsection (e) made, granted, denied, and granted by operation of law, without identifying any applicant. Publication under this subsection identifies no person and states no fact about any application other than its disposition.\n\n**(f) The exemption is tailoring, not a cure.** The exemption in subsection (e), and any confidentiality afforded to information filed under this Section, are not offered in justification of the disclosure obligation. That obligation rests on the informational interest of the electorate in knowing who assembles the funds on which a candidacy depends, recognized in Buckley v. Valeo and sustained in Citizens United v. FEC, and on the narrow scope of what subsections (a) through (d) require."},{"anchor":"sec-2-1b","heading":"\u00a7 2-1B. Paid Promotion","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 DISCLOSURE AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) The rule.** A communication to which this Section applies shall carry the disclosure required by subsection (d), and the compensation for it shall be disclosed under subsection (e).\n\n**(b) Communications to which this Section applies.** This Section applies to a communication published by a promoter where paragraph (1) or paragraph (2) is satisfied.\n\n**(1) Compensation for the communication.** The promoter received compensation from any person in exchange for, on the condition of, or in the expectation of publishing the communication, and the communication refers to a clearly identified candidate for Federal office, or supports or opposes a policy position. A communication does not support or oppose a policy position for purposes of this paragraph merely by referring to the subject of that position.\n\n**(2) Compensation for promotional services.** The promoter received compensation from a covered payer for services that include or contemplate the publication of communications by the promoter, and the communication refers to a clearly identified candidate for Federal office, or supports or opposes a policy position identified in the arrangement under which the compensation is provided. This paragraph applies to a communication published during the period beginning on the date the compensation is agreed to and ending on the date set in the schedule to this Act after the last date on which compensation is provided.\n\n**(2-1) Characterization does not control.** A description of the compensation as being for advice, strategy, consulting, surrogacy, appearance, travel, production, content, media, or any other service does not remove it from paragraph (2) where the services include or contemplate the publication of communications by the promoter. Whether services include or contemplate publication is determined from the terms of the arrangement and from what the promoter in fact does, and not from the description under which the compensation is reported to any authority.\n\n**(2-2) Aggregation.** Compensation provided by two or more persons acting in concert or under common control, and compensation provided by a person together with compensation provided by that person's intermediaries, is treated as provided by one person.\n\n**(2-3) Compensation after publication.** Compensation provided to a person by reason of a communication that person has published is compensation in exchange for that communication for purposes of paragraph (1), and the disclosure required by subsection (d) shall be added to the communication, where it remains available, within 10 days after the compensation is received. Compensation so provided is also compensation for promotional services for purposes of paragraph (2) with respect to each later communication by that person, published within the period under paragraph (2) measured from the date the compensation is provided, that refers to the same candidate or supports or opposes the same policy position.\n\n**(2-4) A later payment closes the gap.** Where a person provides compensation to a promoter after the end of a period under paragraph (2), the period is treated as having continued without interruption from the earlier compensation to the later, and this Section applies to each communication published in the interval as it applies to a communication published within the period. A promoter who adds the disclosure required by subsection (d) to each such communication that remains available within 10 days after receiving the later compensation is not in violation of subsection (d) as to that communication.\n\n**(c) What this Section does not reach.** This Section does not apply to:\n\n**(1)** a communication published by a person who received no compensation to which this Section applies, and nothing in this Section reaches a person publishing on that person's own account and at that person's own expense;\n\n**(2)** a communication published by a person whose only compensation from the payer is for goods or services that neither include nor contemplate the publication of communications by that person, unless subsection (b)(1) applies;\n\n**(3)** a news story, commentary, or editorial published by a person engaged in the regular business of publishing news, where that person received no compensation from a covered payer for its publication and is not owned or controlled by a candidate, a political committee, or a covered political organization;\n\n**(4)** a communication that carries a statement satisfying 52 U.S.C. \u00a7 30120 which states each fact required by subsection (d)(1) of this Section and satisfies subsection (d)(2) of this Section; and\n\n**(5)** a communication where the compensation to which this Section would apply, aggregated under subsection (b)(2-2), does not exceed the amount set in the schedule to this Act for the calendar year, except that this paragraph does not apply where the payer is a candidate or a political committee. The amount is a step: where compensation exceeds it, this Section applies to every communication for which the compensation was provided, and not only to the excess.\n\n**(6) The exceptions are closed.** The exceptions available under this Section are those and only those stated in this subsection. No other exception, exemption, or limitation applies, none is supplied by the common law, and the omission of any subject from this subsection is a determination of the Congress and not a gap.\n\n**(d) The disclosure the communication carries.**\n\n**(1) What it states.** The communication shall state that the promoter was compensated; the name of the person who provided the compensation and, where that person provided it on behalf of another, the name of that other person; and whether the communication was authorized by a candidate or by an authorized committee of a candidate. A statement in substantially the following form satisfies this paragraph: \"Paid promotion. [name] paid me to post this. Not authorized by any candidate or candidate's committee.\"\n\n**(2) Clear and conspicuous.** The disclosure shall be clear and conspicuous. A disclosure is clear and conspicuous for purposes of this Section where it satisfies the standard stated in section 255.0(f) of title 16, Code of Federal Regulations, as that section read on the date of enactment of this Act, without regard to any later amendment, revision, or repeal of that section.\n\n**(3) What does not satisfy paragraph (2).** A disclosure does not satisfy paragraph (2) where it appears only on a profile, channel, or account page rather than with the communication; where it cannot be seen or heard without expanding, scrolling past, clicking, or otherwise acting upon the communication; where it appears only among hashtags, tags, or links; where it appears only in a comment, reply, or description not displayed with the communication; where it is displayed for a period too brief to be read; or where a communication has both a visual and an audible portion and the disclosure is conveyed in only one of them. This paragraph is illustrative and does not limit paragraph (2).\n\n**(4) Platform tools.** A disclosure made using a tool provided by a platform satisfies this subsection only where the resulting disclosure satisfies paragraphs (1) through (3). The availability of such a tool imposes no duty on any platform, and its use is not itself a defense.\n\n**(5) Durability.** For so long as the communication remains available, the disclosure shall remain with it. No person subject to this Section shall remove, obscure, or degrade the disclosure, and no such person is liable under this Section for its removal, obscuring, or degradation by another person.\n\n**(e) The disclosure the payer files.**\n\n**(1) The arrangement.** Within 48 hours of providing compensation to which this Section applies, the person who provided it shall disclose to the Office, in structured, machine-readable form: the identity of the promoter; the amount, date, and form of the compensation; the value of any part of it not provided in money; each intermediary through which it passed; what was purchased; the period during which communications are to be published; and each clearly identified candidate and each policy position to which the arrangement relates.\n\n**(2) The communications.** Within 48 hours of the publication of a communication to which this Section applies, the person who provided the compensation shall disclose the communication to the Office, identified by a reference sufficient to locate it. Where the communication is one to which this Section applies by reason of subsection (b)(2), this paragraph applies only to a communication of which that person has been made aware, and a promoter who notifies that person of a communication discharges no duty of that person under paragraph (1).\n\n**(3) Publication.** The Office shall publish each disclosure under this subsection under \u00a7 2-1(c).\n\n**(f) Notice, as a condition of the payment.** No person shall provide compensation to which this Section applies without giving the promoter, in writing and before or with the compensation, notice stating that the communication must carry the disclosure required by subsection (d), the name to be stated under subsection (d)(1), and whether the communication is authorized by a candidate or by an authorized committee of a candidate. The duty under this subsection runs to the person providing the compensation and to each intermediary that transmits it.\n\n**(g) Intermediaries.**\n\n**(1)** A person does not discharge any duty under this Section by providing compensation through an intermediary.\n\n**(2)** An intermediary that transmits compensation to which this Section applies shall transmit with it the information the intermediary holds identifying the person on whose behalf it is transmitted, together with the notice required by subsection (f). This paragraph requires the transmission of information the intermediary already holds and requires no new collection.\n\n**(3)** Where an intermediary does not transmit the identity of the person on whose behalf it acts, the intermediary is the person who provided the compensation for purposes of subsections (d), (e), and (f), and remains liable for the failure to transmit.\n\n**(h) Foreign payers.** Where the person who provided the compensation, or the person on whose behalf it was provided, is a foreign national within the meaning of 52 U.S.C. \u00a7 30121(b) or a foreign principal within the meaning of section 1(b) of the Foreign Agents Registration Act of 1938 (22 U.S.C. \u00a7 611(b)), the disclosure required by subsection (d) shall so state. Nothing in this subsection permits any payment prohibited by 52 U.S.C. \u00a7 30121.\n\n**(i) Consequence.**\n\n**(1) The promise is unenforceable.** A promise to provide compensation to which this Section applies is unenforceable by the promoter unless the communication carried the disclosure required by subsection (d) when it was published, or the person who provided the compensation failed to give the notice required by subsection (f). Compensation provided for a communication that did not carry that disclosure is not recoverable by the person who provided it. A court of the United States or of a State decides any question arising under this paragraph, notwithstanding any agreement to the contrary, including an agreement to arbitrate and any provision purporting to delegate that question to an arbitrator.\n\n**(2) Civil consequence.** A violation of this Section is subject to disgorgement to the Treasury of the compensation provided or received in violation of this Section, together with a civil penalty in an equal amount, and a published finding of violation. Each amount is determined by a court of the United States. An amount disgorged or paid as a penalty under this paragraph is not deductible for any tax purpose, and no such amount is treated as restitution, remediation, or an amount paid to come into compliance with law for purposes of section 162(f)(2) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986.\n\n**(3) Who bears it.** The person who provided the compensation is liable under paragraph (2) without regard to intent. A promoter is liable under paragraph (2) only where the promoter received the notice required by subsection (f) or otherwise knew of the duty imposed by subsection (d).\n\n**(4) Cure.** Section 2-5(c) applies to a failure under this Section. A promoter who adds the disclosure required by subsection (d) to a communication that remains available, within 10 days after the failure is known or reasonably should be known, is not subject to a civil penalty for that failure, and the compensation is enforceable and not disgorgeable by reason of that failure.\n\n**(j) Threat of harassment.** Subsections (e) through (f) of \u00a7 2-1A apply to the disclosure of the name of a person who provided compensation under this Section as they apply to a disclosure under that Section. Where an exemption is granted, the disclosure required by subsection (d) shall state that the communication was paid for and that the identity of the payer is withheld under an exemption granted by the Office, together with the identifying number of the exemption. No exemption excuses the statement that the communication was paid for.\n\n**(k) No limit on speech.** Nothing in this Section limits any communication, the content of any communication, the amount any person may spend or receive, or the number of communications any person may publish.\n\n**(k-1) Platforms: the two duties.**\n\n**(1) Non-removal.** A platform shall not remove, obscure, suppress, or degrade a disclosure required by subsection (d) that is carried by a communication published through its service. A platform does not violate this paragraph by removing or declining to publish the communication itself, or by any change applied to communications generally rather than to the disclosure.\n\n**(2) Preservation of what the platform already holds.** Where a platform maintains a record that a communication was published as a paid partnership, as sponsored content, or as the equivalent, the platform shall preserve that record for the period set in the schedule to this Act and shall produce it to the Office on a request that identifies a communication which is the subject of a complaint the Office has determined under \u00a7 2-5(a) states probable cause, or of an action under \u00a7 2-2 or \u00a7 2-2A. The Office shall make no other request under this paragraph. This paragraph requires the preservation of a record the platform already creates. It requires no new collection, no identification of any communication, and no judgment as to whether this Section applies to any communication.\n\n**(k-2) Nothing further is required of a platform or a publisher.** Except as provided in \u00a7 2-1B(k-1), this Section imposes no duty on a platform, on any other person who provides a service through which communications are published, or on a person engaged in the regular business of publishing news, with respect to a communication published by another person. No such person is required to identify, review, judge, label, collect, publish, or verify any communication or any information under this Section, and no consequence under this Act attaches to any such person for a communication published by another.\n\n**(k-3) What a platform is answerable for.** A failure to comply with \u00a7 2-1B(k-1) is subject to an action under \u00a7 2-2A to compel compliance. No disgorgement and no civil penalty under subsection (i)(2) attaches to a platform, to any other person who provides a service through which communications are published, or to a person engaged in the regular business of publishing news.\n\n**(l) The authority for each application.** As applied to a communication referring to a clearly identified candidate for Federal office, this Section is enacted under the authority of Congress over Federal elections, including article I, section 4, and article I, section 8, clause 18. As applied to a communication supporting or opposing a policy position, this Section is enacted under article I, section 8, clause 3, and under the authority of Congress to require the disclosure of paid efforts to influence the Congress and the Government of the United States. As applied to a communication paid for by a foreign national or a foreign principal, this Section is enacted under the authority of Congress over foreign affairs, foreign commerce, and the participation of foreign nationals in Federal elections. Each application rests on its own authority and operates whether or not any other application operates."},{"anchor":"sec-2-1c","heading":"\u00a7 2-1C. Accounts Operated by a Covered Payer","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 DISCLOSURE AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) The rule.** An account operated by a covered payer shall identify the covered payer as subsection (c) provides, and a change in the control of an account shall be disclosed as subsection (d) provides.\n\n**(b) Accounts to which this Section applies.** This Section applies to an account operated by a covered payer through which a communication referring to a clearly identified candidate for Federal office is published. It applies whether the covered payer established the account or acquired it, and whether the account is held in the name of the covered payer, of a natural person, of a publication, or of any other name. It applies also, as subsection (j) provides, to a funded account described in that subsection.\n\n**(c) The identification.**\n\n**(1) The account.** The account shall state, in the description, profile, or equivalent visible to any person viewing the account without further action, the name of the covered payer that operates it. An account held in the name of the covered payer satisfies this paragraph.\n\n**(2) The communication.** Where the name in which the account is held does not state the name of the covered payer, each communication published through the account that refers to a clearly identified candidate for Federal office shall state that it is published by the covered payer, naming it, in a manner satisfying \u00a7 2-1B(d)(2), and \u00a7 2-1B(d)(3) applies. A communication carrying a statement satisfying 52 U.S.C. \u00a7 30120, or a disclosure under \u00a7 2-1B(d), that names the covered payer satisfies this paragraph.\n\n**(3) Durability.** \u00a7 2-1B(d)(5) applies to a statement under this subsection.\n\n**(d) Change of control.**\n\n**(1) Disclosure filed.** Within 48 hours after a covered payer acquires the operation of an account previously operated by another person, the covered payer shall disclose to the Office, in structured, machine-readable form: the account; the person from whom it was acquired; the date; the consideration provided, with the fair market value of any part of it not provided in money; and each intermediary through which the consideration passed. The Office shall publish each disclosure under \u00a7 2-1(c).\n\n**(2) Statement on the account.** For the period set in the schedule to this Act after the acquisition, the statement under subsection (c)(1) shall also state the date on which the covered payer acquired the operation of the account.\n\n**(3) Consideration to the transferor.** Consideration provided to the transferor of an account is compensation to which \u00a7 2-1B applies with respect to each communication the transferor publishes, through any account, within the period under \u00a7 2-1B(b)(2) measured from the date the consideration is provided, that refers to a clearly identified candidate for Federal office or supports or opposes a policy position identified in the arrangement.\n\n**(e) What this Section does not reach.** This Section does not apply to:\n\n**(1)** an account operated by a person who is not a covered payer, other than a funded account under subsection (j), and nothing in this Section reaches a person who is not a covered payer publishing on that person's own account and at that person's own expense;\n\n**(2)** a communication that does not refer to a clearly identified candidate for Federal office; or\n\n**(3)** an account on which the covered payer's only involvement is the provision of compensation to a promoter, which \u00a7 2-1B governs, or of funds to which subsection (j) applies.\n\n**(4) The exceptions are closed.** The exceptions available under this Section are those and only those stated in this subsection. No other exception, exemption, or limitation applies, none is supplied by the common law, and the omission of any subject from this subsection is a determination of the Congress and not a gap. An account is not excepted from this Section by reason that it publishes news, commentary, or editorial content.\n\n**(f) Consequence.**\n\n**(1) Civil consequence.** A failure to comply with this Section is subject to an action under \u00a7 2-2A to compel compliance, and to disgorgement to the Treasury of the amount expended by the covered payer to acquire, operate, staff, supply, or promote the account during the period of noncompliance, together with a civil penalty in an equal amount, and a published finding of violation. Each amount is determined by a court of the United States. The second sentence of \u00a7 2-1B(i)(2) applies to an amount under this paragraph. Liability under this paragraph does not require proof of intent.\n\n**(2) Who bears it.** The covered payer bears the consequence under paragraph (1), and no natural person bears it by reason only of having acted as its officer, employee, agent, or contractor; except that where the covered payer has been dissolved or is without assets sufficient to satisfy the amount, each person who directed the operation of the account bears it.\n\n**(3) Cure.** Section 2-5(c) applies to a failure under this Section. A covered payer that brings an account into compliance with subsection (c) within 10 days after the failure is known or reasonably should be known is not subject to a civil penalty for that failure.\n\n**(g) Threat of harassment.** \u00a7 2-1B(j) applies to the name of a covered payer under this Section, and to the name of a covered political organization under subsection (j), as it applies to the name of a person who provided compensation under that Section, and no exemption excuses the statement that the account is operated by a covered payer or funded by a covered political organization.\n\n**(h) Nothing further is required; no limit on speech.** \u00a7 2-1B(k-2) and (k-3) apply to an account and a communication under this Section as they apply to a communication under \u00a7 2-1B. Nothing in this Section limits any communication, the content of any communication, the number of accounts a covered payer may operate, or the amount any person may spend.\n\n**(i) The authority.** This Section is enacted under the authority of Congress over Federal elections, including article I, section 4, and article I, section 8, clause 18, and serves the interest stated in \u00a7 0-2(11).\n\n**(j) Funded accounts.**\n\n**(1) The account.** An account is a funded account during a calendar quarter where, in the four calendar quarters preceding it, the person that operates the account received from one covered political organization, together with the persons under common control with that organization, funds exceeding the amount set in the schedule to this Act, and a communication referring to a clearly identified candidate for Federal office is published through the account during that quarter. Funds include a grant, a contribution, a loan, an investment, a payment, and anything else of value, in whatever form and however described, other than a payment for advertising or services at rates and on terms available to the general public. The amount is a step: where funds exceed it, this subsection applies to the account, and not only in respect of the excess.\n\n**(2) The statement.** A funded account shall state, in the manner required by subsection (c)(1), the name of the covered political organization and that it provided funds to the person that operates the account. Where more than one organization satisfies paragraph (1), the statement names each.\n\n**(3) Funding is not operation.** Funds described in paragraph (1) do not by themselves make the covered political organization the operator of the account, and subsection (c)(2) does not apply to a communication by reason only of this subsection. Where the organization also operates the account, subsection (c) applies.\n\n**(4) Consequence.** A failure to comply with paragraph (2) is subject to an action under \u00a7 2-2A to compel compliance, and to a civil penalty, determined by a court of the United States, not exceeding the funds received from the organization during the period of noncompliance. No disgorgement attaches under this subsection. Paragraph (3) of subsection (f) applies to a failure under this subsection as it applies to a failure to comply with subsection (c).\n\n**(5) Who bears it.** The person that operates the account bears the consequence under paragraph (4). No consequence under this subsection attaches to the covered political organization by reason of the funds it provided, and nothing in this subsection limits the funds any person may provide or receive."},{"anchor":"sec-2-2","heading":"\u00a7 2-2. Civil Enforcement","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 DISCLOSURE AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a)** A violation of Section 1 is subject to disgorgement to the Treasury of the full amount of value that flowed to the candidate, covered person, or family member in violation of this Act, together with a civil penalty in an equal amount, and a published finding of violation. Disgorgement is payable personally by the recipient and not from any political committee or covered political organization.\n\n**(b)** Liability under this Section does not require proof of intent.\n\n**(c)** The Attorney General brings a civil action under this Section in a court of the United States on the referral of the Office. The Office does not itself bring any action under this Act.\n\n**(c-1) Deemed referral.** A complaint is a referral by the Office, for purposes of subsection (c), of the conduct the complaint alleges, effective on the first of the following to occur: a deadlocked vote of the Office on the complaint, or a failure to obtain the votes required to proceed on it; the issuance of a writing on the complaint that \u00a7 2-2A(a-1) treats as a declination; the expiration of the period in \u00a7 2-2A(a) without a civil action under this Section commenced on the complaint and without a written finding resolving it; and the complaint's being deemed declined under \u00a7 2-5(b-1). No later act or finding of the Office withdraws or limits a referral under this subsection. Within 10 days after the declination, the Office shall transmit the complaint and the record of its handling to the Attorney General; the referral does not depend on the transmittal.\n\n**(c-2) Fallback.** If the requirement of subsection (c) that an action be brought on the referral of the Office is held invalid, in whole or as applied, the Attorney General may bring a civil action under this Section without a referral, in every case or in the cases to which the holding applies."},{"anchor":"sec-2-2a","heading":"\u00a7 2-2A. Enforcement Failure and Action to Compel Disclosure","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 DISCLOSURE AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Deadlock or inaction.** Where the Office fails to act, a complaint presented to it is deemed declined if, within 120 days of presentation, no civil action under \u00a7 2-2 has been commenced on the complaint and the Office has not made a written finding resolving it. A deadlocked vote, a failure to obtain the votes required to proceed, and the expiration of the period without action each constitute a declination.\n\n**(a-1) What a written finding must contain.** A written finding satisfies subsection (a) only where it states the facts relied upon, the provision of this Act considered, and the basis for declining to proceed. A writing that does not is a declination for purposes of subsection (a), whatever it is titled.\n\n**(b) Action to compel disclosure.** Upon a declination, any person who has been denied information to which this Act entitles the public may bring a civil action in a United States district court to compel the filing, correction, or publication of a disclosure required by Section 2 or by \u00a7 1-7, and to obtain a declaration that a required disclosure was not made.\n\n**(c) The injury.** This Act entitles the public to the information required to be disclosed under Section 2 and \u00a7 1-7. A person denied that information is injured by the denial, and the injury is concrete: without it the person cannot determine whether a candidate is profiting personally from the funds raised for the candidacy, who has assembled those funds, who paid for a communication addressed to that person, or who operates the account through which a communication about a candidate reached that person.\n\n**(d) Remedies.** The court may order the filing, correction, or publication of a disclosure, may enter a declaration that a required disclosure was not made, and may award reasonable attorney's fees and costs to a prevailing plaintiff. The court may not award damages, disgorgement, or a civil penalty in an action under this Section.\n\n**(e) Fee-shifting, both ways.** A prevailing plaintiff is entitled to reasonable attorney's fees and costs. Where the court finds the action frivolous, unreasonable, or brought in bad faith, the plaintiff is liable for the defendant's reasonable fees and costs.\n\n**(f) Public-information bar.** An action may not be based solely on information already published.\n\n**(g) No proof of legislative acts.** An action under this Section may not require proof of a legislative act of a Member of Congress and abrogates no privilege.\n\n**(h) Declination published.** Where a plaintiff obtains a declaration under subsection (d) and no civil action under \u00a7 2-2 is commenced with respect to the same conduct within the limitations period, the Office shall publish that fact. Nothing in this subsection compels any enforcement decision.\n\n**(h-1) Concurrent jurisdiction; removal barred.** **(1)** An action under this Section may be brought in a district court of the United States **or in any State court of competent jurisdiction.** **(2)** An action under this Section brought in a State court **may not be removed to a district court of the United States.** **(3)** A State court shall not decline to hear an action under this Section on the ground that it arises under Federal law, nor dismiss it on the ground that the plaintiff would lack standing in a court of the United States. **(4)** A person is not required to proceed under the subsection providing for partial assignment of the claim of the United States unless an action under this subsection has first been dismissed or is unavailable.\n\n**(i) Alternative structure preserved on adverse holding.** If an action under this Section is held not to present a case or controversy as to any plaintiff or claim, then as to that plaintiff or claim a person may bring an action to enforce Section 1 or Section 2 as a partial assignee of the claim of the United States, receiving a share of not less than 15 and not more than 25 percent of any amount actually recovered, which is paid otherwise to the Treasury, subject to subsections (e) through (g)."},{"anchor":"sec-2-2b","heading":"\u00a7 2-2B. Venue, Limitations, and Cooperation","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 DISCLOSURE AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Venue.** A civil action or criminal prosecution under this Act may be brought in the United States District Court for the District of Columbia, or in the district in which the candidate resides, in which the committee or organization maintains its principal place of business, or in which the conduct occurred.\n\n**(b) Limitations.** An action or prosecution under this Act must be commenced within six years after the later of the violation or its discovery by the Office or the Department of Justice. The period is tolled during any time the person cannot be prosecuted, and does not expire before the end of the second year after the candidate leaves any office to which the candidacy led.\n\n**(c) Cooperation.** On the written recommendation of the Office, a court determining a civil penalty under this Act may reduce or waive the penalty for a person who has provided material assistance in an action or prosecution under this Act against another person, arising from a violation in which the person providing the assistance took part. The recommendation states the assistance provided and does not bind the court. The Office does not itself reduce, waive, or determine any civil penalty. The Department of Justice may grant immunity or a charging or sentencing concession as to criminal liability under existing law. The Office does not grant immunity from criminal prosecution."},{"anchor":"sec-2-2c","heading":"\u00a7 2-2C. Defenses and Construction","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 DISCLOSURE AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Defenses exclusive.** The defenses, immunities, exceptions, and limitations on liability available in an action or proceeding under this Act are those and only those expressly stated in this Act. The common law supplies none. **No defense is to be inferred from the omission of any subject from this Act.**\n\n**(b) No reconstitution.** A defense or limitation omitted from this Act may not be reintroduced as an element of a plaintiff's case, a pleading requirement, a standard of proof, or a prudential limitation on jurisdiction.\n\n**(c) No channeling.** The receipt, screening, publication, or referral of a filing by the Office is not a remedy and does not channel, preclude, or displace any action under this Act. No statute establishing a remedial scheme shall be construed to displace a remedy provided by this Act unless it so provides by express reference to this Act. **Nothing in this subsection limits \u00a7 2-2A(a)**, which conditions an action to compel disclosure on a declination.\n\n**(d) Mootness.** The termination of a candidacy, the dissolution of a committee, the correction of a filing, or the cessation of a practice after the commencement of an action does not render the action moot as to any period during which the obligation was unmet. A person asserting mootness bears the burden of establishing that the conduct cannot reasonably be expected to recur.\n\n**(e) Pleading.** A complaint alleging that a required filing was not made, was incomplete, or was false states a claim. **No heightened pleading requirement applies.** Knowledge and intent may be alleged generally and proved by circumstantial evidence.\n\n**(f) No integrated scheme.** Nothing in this Section shall be cited as evidence that the provisions of this Act constitute a single design. Each remedy rests on its own authority and operates whether or not any other is available or valid."},{"anchor":"sec-2-2d","heading":"\u00a7 2-2D. Annual test of what was filed","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 DISCLOSURE AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Duty.** The Office shall test annually whether the disclosures filed under this Act are complete on their face and consistent with information the Office already holds, applying a published method, and shall publish the results in aggregate.\n\n**(b) What is published.** The number of filings tested, the number found facially incomplete, and the number in which reported disbursements were inconsistent with information otherwise available to the Office.\n\n**(c) The consequence of not testing is the consequence the Act already provides.** Where the Office has not published a test for a period within 120 days of the end of that period, **the failure is an inaction for purposes of \u00a7 2-2A**, and the remedies of that Section apply.\n\n**(d) No new enforcement power.** This Section confers no authority to impose a penalty, and no test under it is a finding that any provision has been violated. **Penalties remain for a court to set.**"},{"anchor":"sec-2-3","heading":"\u00a7 2-3. Criminal Liability","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 DISCLOSURE AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a)** It is a felony, punishable by imprisonment of not more than five years and forfeiture, to:\n\n1. willfully file a false certification or disclosure under this Act; or\n\n2. conceal or misrepresent an arrangement to evade this Act, including through an undisclosed nominee, a sham entity, a layered arrangement used to obscure a beneficial interest, or a backdated or sham transaction.\n\n**(b)** Criminal liability under this Act rests only on the discrete acts described in subsection (a), each provable without inquiry into how any Member of Congress legislated. No criminal liability arises from the attribution standard of \u00a7 1-4 or from any assessment of a payment's fair market value."},{"anchor":"sec-2-3a","heading":"\u00a7 2-3A. Whistleblower Award","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 DISCLOSURE AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a)** Where the United States recovers a civil penalty or disgorgement under this Act based on information provided by an original source, the Attorney General shall pay that source an award of a percentage, set in the schedule to this Act, of the amount actually recovered, paid from the recovery.\n\n**(b)** No award is payable under this Section in respect of an amount recovered in an action brought under \u00a7 2-2A."},{"anchor":"sec-2-4","heading":"\u00a7 2-4. Safe Harbor","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 DISCLOSURE AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"A candidate, committee, or organization that fully and accurately discloses an arrangement and obtains a written ruling of compliance from the Office is immune from criminal liability under \u00a7 2-3 with respect to that arrangement as disclosed. The immunity does not extend to civil disgorgement, does not survive a material omission or misrepresentation in the disclosure on which the ruling was based, and does not bar prosecution for an act described in \u00a7 2-3(a). Each ruling is published and precedential.\n\n**Deadline.** The Office shall issue or decline a ruling within the period set in the schedule to this Act after a complete disclosure. A ruling not issued within the period is not granted by operation of law; where none has issued, the Office shall publish the fact of the delay and the expected date of issuance, and shall republish it every 30 days until the ruling issues or is declined.\n\n**Annual report.** The Office shall publish annually a report of every ruling issued under this Section, including the arrangement approved and the reasoning, together with aggregate statistics on rulings sought, granted, and denied."},{"anchor":"sec-2-4a","heading":"\u00a7 2-4A. The Office Exercises No Executive Power","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 DISCLOSURE AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a)** Under this Act the Office receives filings, screens complaints under \u00a7 2-5(a), publishes, maintains the database required by \u00a7 2-1(c), issues rulings under \u00a7 2-4, makes referrals, and makes the recommendations described in \u00a7 2-2B(c). The Office shall not impose or determine any disgorgement, penalty, or other monetary consequence, shall not adjudicate any claim of violation, and shall not compel any person. Every monetary remedy under this Act is determined by a court of the United States in an action under \u00a7 2-2 or \u00a7 2-2A.\n\n**(b) No protection from removal is required or conferred.** Because the Office exercises no part of the executive power of the United States under this Act, nothing in this Act depends upon any protection of the tenure of any Commissioner, and this Act neither confers nor relies upon any such protection.\n\n**(c) Fallback.** If any statutory protection of the tenure of a member of the Federal Election Commission is held invalid, or if the composition or structure of the Commission is altered by any means, the Commission continues to perform the functions described in subsection (a), and \u00a7 2-2A operates on the same terms."},{"anchor":"sec-2-5","heading":"\u00a7 2-5. Administration","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 DISCLOSURE AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Screening of complaints.** The Office shall not publish or treat a complaint as a finding, and shall not make a complaint public in a manner identifying its subject, until the Office has determined that the complaint states probable cause to believe a violation occurred. A complaint failing that threshold is dismissed.\n\n**(a-1) This Section restricts the Office alone.** Subsection (a) governs publication by the Office and by no other person. It imposes no obligation of confidentiality on a complainant, a witness, a candidate, a committee, or any member of the public; it does not restrict the press; and nothing in this Act prevents any person from publishing a complaint, its contents, or the fact that it was filed.\n\n**(b) Expedited handling near an election.** The Office shall establish expedited procedures for a complaint presented within 60 days of an election, so that a meritorious complaint is resolved in time to inform the public and a meritless one is dismissed before it can be used as a weapon.\n\n**(b-1) The absence of procedures does not suspend the handling.** Until procedures under subsection (b) are established and published, a complaint presented within 60 days of an election that is neither resolved nor the subject of a civil action commenced under \u00a7 2-2 within the period set in the schedule to this Act is deemed declined for purposes of \u00a7 2-2A, and that Section applies according to its terms.\n\n**(c) Cure of good-faith error.** A person who, in good faith, files an incomplete or erroneous disclosure and corrects it within 30 days after the error is known or reasonably should be known is not subject to civil penalty for the error, though the underlying obligation to disclose and the prohibition on the underlying conduct continue. This cure does not excuse an act described in \u00a7 2-3(a).\n\n**(d) Free filing tools.** The Office shall provide, without charge, standardized, machine-readable software and forms for all filings required under this Act, designed to prevent common errors. The availability of conforming free tools is relevant to whether an error was made in good faith under subsection (c).\n\n**(e) Aggregate compliance reporting.** The Office shall publish annually aggregate statistics on compliance under this Act, including filings, timeliness, complaints received, screened, and sustained, enforcement actions and outcomes, amounts recovered, and the number of matters deemed declined under \u00a7 2-2A(a); **and the time from a complete request to the issuance of an opinion under \u00a7 2-4, from an application under \u00a7 2-1A(e) to its grant or denial, and from presentation of a complaint to its screening under \u00a7 2-5, each at the median and at the ninetieth percentile.** **Where those times exceed the periods set in the schedule in more than the share set in the schedule, that is evidence that the Office's staffing is inadequate and shall be stated as such in the publication**, together with **the personnel required to meet them**, transmitted to the Congress and renewed annually until they are met.\n\n**(f) Due process.** Before publishing a determination under subsection (a) that a complaint states probable cause, the Office shall give the person written notice of the provision alleged and the conduct at issue, access to the evidence relied on, and an opportunity to respond in writing and in person. A determination of the Office is subject to judicial review. The Office imposes no civil consequence under this Act; every monetary remedy is determined by a court, as \u00a7 2-4A provides."},{"anchor":"sec-3-1","heading":"\u00a7 3-1. Relationship to Existing Law","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Floor, not ceiling.** No provision of this Act reduces, repeals, or supersedes any prohibition or penalty of existing law, including the personal-use prohibition of 52 U.S.C. \u00a7 30114(b). Where existing law is stricter, it continues to apply; where this Act is stricter, it governs.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 3 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-3-1a","heading":"\u00a7 3-1A. Self-Executing Obligations and Independence","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Self-executing.** The following obligations operate without any determination, election, or action by any officer of the United States, and no officer may suspend, waive, or decline to give them effect: the disclosure duties of \u00a7 2-1, \u00a7 2-1A, and \u00a7 2-1B; the identification and disclosure duties of \u00a7 2-1C; the salary cap of \u00a7 1-2(b); the prohibitions of \u00a7 1-3, \u00a7 1-8, and \u00a7 1-10; the disclosure duty of \u00a7 1-10(e); the notice required by \u00a7 2-1B(f); and the royalty-free license of \u00a7 1-3(c). The failure of any officer to act does not excuse, toll, or diminish any of them.\n\n**(b) Publication ministerial.** The publication required by \u00a7 2-1(c) is a ministerial duty, enforceable by mandamus at the suit of any person, and no discretion attaches to it.\n\n**(b-1) No waiver.** No officer of the United States may suspend, waive, or decline to give effect to any obligation described in subsection (a), and no such action is effective. The failure of the Office or of any other authority to act does not excuse, toll, or diminish any such obligation, and compliance is not conditioned on any authority having acted.\n\n**(c) Independence from the other Acts.** No provision of this Act depends for its operation or validity on the enactment or continued effect of the People's Prosperity Act, the Executive Integrity Act, the Congressional Integrity Act, or the Judiciary Integrity Act. Where this Act refers to a definition or standard in another Act and that Act is not in effect, the definition or standard is applied as it read on the date of enactment of this Act.\n\n**(d) No insulated enforcer.** This Act confers no power on any body that would require protection from removal. The Office receives, publishes, screens, and refers. Every financial remedy under this Act is determined by a court of the United States."},{"anchor":"sec-3-2","heading":"\u00a7 3-2. Effective Date","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a)** This Act takes effect for candidacies begun after the first regularly scheduled Federal general election following enactment.\n\n**(b)** No provision of this Act varying the compensation of Senators or Representatives takes effect until an election of Representatives has intervened."},{"anchor":"sec-3-3","heading":"\u00a7 3-3. Severability","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"If any provision of this Act, or its application to any person or circumstance, is held invalid, the remainder of this Act and its application to other persons or circumstances are not affected. Each Section, and each subsection of Section 1, is severable from every other.\n\n**(0) No scheme; construction against aggregation.** This Act establishes no scheme of regulation of political speech, association, or expenditure. Each obligation it imposes is a discrete rule of conduct or disclosure, stands independently of every other, and is complete in itself. Congress would have enacted each obligation of this Act standing alone, in the absence of every other, and directs that each be so construed and, if necessary, so preserved. No provision shall be construed as part of a larger scheme by reason of its enactment in the same statute as any other provision, and a holding that any provision or group of provisions is invalid shall not be extended to any other provision on the ground that the provisions together constitute a scheme, a design, or a pattern.\n\n**(a) Severability by provision.** The candidate-salary rule of \u00a7 1-2, the self-dealing prohibitions of \u00a7 1-3, the serving-official rule of \u00a7 1-5, the speech-preservation rule of \u00a7 1-6, and the own-election position rule of \u00a7 1-10 are each independently severable, so that invalidation of one does not affect the others. Within \u00a7 1-10, the prohibition of subsection (a) and the disclosure duty of subsection (e) are severable from each other, and if the prohibition is held invalid as to any position, the disclosure duty of subsection (e) applies to that position. Within \u00a7 1-3, the flat prohibition of subsection (a) and the capped-and-certified family rule of subsection (d) are severable from each other and from the remaining subsections.\n\n**(b) Severability of each restriction as applied to family members.** Each restriction of this Act, to the extent it applies to a family member, is individually severable from the same restriction as applied to a candidate or covered person, and from every other restriction as applied to a family member. If the application of any restriction to family members is held invalid, in whole or as to any class of family member, that restriction remains in force as applied to candidates and covered persons, and every other restriction remains in force as applied to family members.\n\n**(c) Severability of each entity's coverage.** The application of this Act to each type of covered political organization is severable from its application to every other type, so that if coverage of any one type is held invalid, coverage of the others is not affected.\n\n**(d) Severability of the related-expenditure disclosure.** The disclosure obligation of \u00a7 1-7 is severable from every other provision. If it is held invalid in whole or in any application, including on the ground that requiring disclosure of an organization's disbursements burdens independent expenditure, the disclosure of the payment itself under \u00a7 1-7(a) survives as to the payment, the recipient, and its beneficial owners; the self-dealing prohibitions of \u00a7 1-3 remain in force; and nothing in this Act limits any disbursement.\n\n**(d-1) Severability of the enforcement provisions.** The action to compel disclosure of \u00a7 2-2A, the alternative structure of \u00a7 2-2A(i), the venue and limitations provisions of \u00a7 2-2B, and each subsection of \u00a7 2-5 are each severable from one another and from the substantive prohibitions of Section 1. If the action to compel disclosure is held not to present a case or controversy, \u00a7 2-2A(i) applies; if that structure is also held invalid, enforcement by the Attorney General under \u00a7 2-2 continues and the substantive prohibitions remain in force. Congress would have enacted each structure in the absence of the other.\n\n**(d-2) Severability of the Office's structure from the Act's substance.** The provisions describing the functions of the Office are severable from every substantive rule. If any protection of the tenure of a Commissioner is held invalid, or the Commission's structure is altered, the prohibitions of Section 1, the disclosure obligations of Section 2, the action to compel disclosure under \u00a7 2-2A, and every remedy determined by a court remain in full force.\n\n**(d-3) Severability of \u00a7 1-5.** Subsection (a) of \u00a7 1-5 is severable from subsection (b), and the prohibition on retainers, consulting arrangements, and salary is severable from the prohibition on compensation for services related to official duties or electoral activity.\n\n**(e) Severability of the disclosure provisions.** The bundler disclosure of \u00a7 2-1A, the paid-promotion disclosure of \u00a7 2-1B, the account identification of \u00a7 2-1C, the fund disclosure of \u00a7 1-9(c), and the safe-harbor report of \u00a7 2-4 are each severable from one another and from the substantive prohibitions of this Act. If a disclosure requirement is held invalid, including on the ground that it burdens associational privacy, the prohibitions of Section 1 remain in force.\n\n**(e-1) Severability within \u00a7 2-1B.** The application of \u00a7 2-1B to a communication referring to a clearly identified candidate for Federal office is severable from its application to a communication supporting or opposing a policy position, and each is severable from its application to a communication paid for by a foreign national or a foreign principal; if any application is held invalid, the others remain in force. Within \u00a7 2-1B, the disclosure carried by the communication under subsection (d), the disclosure filed by the payer under subsection (e), the notice required by subsection (f), the intermediary rules of subsection (g), and the unenforceability provided by subsection (i)(1) are each severable from one another. If subsection (d) is held invalid as to any class of communication, subsections (e), (f), and (g) apply to that class. If subsection (b)(2) is held invalid, subsection (b)(1) remains in force. The duties imposed on a platform by \u00a7 2-1B(k-1) are severable from every other provision of \u00a7 2-1B and from each other; if either is held invalid, including on the ground that it burdens a platform's editorial discretion, every other obligation of \u00a7 2-1B remains in force as to payers, promoters, and intermediaries.\n\n**(e-2) Severability of the policy-position application by class.** Within the application of \u00a7 2-1B to a communication supporting or opposing a policy position, the application to a communication supporting or opposing a policy position on a pending decision is severable from the application to a communication supporting or opposing any other policy position. If the application to a communication supporting or opposing a policy position that is not a policy position on a pending decision is held invalid, in whole or as to any class of communication or payer, \u00a7 2-1B continues to apply to a communication supporting or opposing a policy position on a pending decision, and Congress would have enacted that application standing alone. The definition in \u00a7 0-3(n) is not narrowed by that holding for any other purpose.\n\n**(e-3) Severability within \u00a7 2-1C.** The application of \u00a7 2-1C to a political committee is severable from its application to a covered political organization that is not a political committee. Within \u00a7 2-1C, the identification of the account under subsection (c)(1), the identification of the communication under subsection (c)(2), and the change-of-control disclosure under subsection (d) are each severable from one another. If subsection (c)(2) is held invalid as to any class of covered payer, subsections (c)(1) and (d) apply to that class; if subsection (c) is held invalid as to any class, subsection (d) applies to that class. The funded-account statement of \u00a7 2-1C(j) is severable from every other provision of \u00a7 2-1C and from the definition of operate in \u00a7 0-3(p); if it is held invalid, in whole or as to any class of account, every other provision of \u00a7 2-1C remains in force, and if any other provision of \u00a7 2-1C is held invalid, subsection (j) remains in force."},{"anchor":"sec-3-4","heading":"\u00a7 3-4. Resources and Continuity of Operations","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Authorization.** There are authorized to be appropriated, for each fiscal year, such sums as are necessary to carry out this Act, including: the receipt, screening, and publication of disclosures under Section 2, including \u00a7 2-1B(e) and \u00a7 2-1C(d); the database required by \u00a7 2-1(c); the free filing tools required by \u00a7 2-5(d); the expedited handling of complaints near an election under \u00a7 2-5(b); and the issuance and publication of rulings under \u00a7 2-4.\n\n**(b) No-year availability.** Amounts appropriated under subsection (a) are available until expended.\n\n**(b-1) Permanent appropriation for the self-executing core.** There are appropriated, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, such sums as are necessary for the receipt and preservation of disclosures under \u00a7 2-1, \u00a7 2-1A, \u00a7 2-1B(e), and \u00a7 2-1C(d) and for the publication required by \u00a7 2-1(c). Amounts under this subsection are appropriated permanently and indefinitely, are available without further appropriation and without fiscal-year limitation, and do not lapse. **The duties \u00a7 3-1A declares self-executing and ministerial do not depend on an annual appropriation.**\n\n**(c) No obligation without appropriation.** **Except for the duties funded by subsection (b-1), which are effective in every fiscal year without regard to this subsection**, no duty imposed by this Act on any agency is effective in a fiscal year except to the extent that amounts are appropriated to carry it out. Where amounts are insufficient, the agency shall perform the duties in the order of priority in subsection (d), shall publish a statement identifying each duty not performed and the amount required to perform it, and shall transmit that statement to the Congress. No penalty, finding, or consequence attaches to an agency, officer, or employee for a duty not performed for want of appropriation.\n\n**(c-1) This subsection does not qualify \u00a7 3-1A.** No obligation described in \u00a7 3-1A(a), and no ministerial duty described in \u00a7 3-1A(b), is excused, tolled, suspended, or rendered ineffective by the absence or insufficiency of an appropriation, by a failure to request one, or by a failure to apportion, allot, or obligate amounts appropriated. **A defense that the duty was not effective, or was excused, for want of funds is not available in any action to enforce an obligation or duty described in \u00a7 3-1A**, whether brought under \u00a7 3-1A(b), \u00a7 2-2A, or any other provision of law.\n\n**(d) Order of priority.** Where amounts appropriated under subsection (a) are insufficient to perform every duty **other than those funded by subsection (b-1)**, they shall be performed in the following order: first, the receipt and preservation of filings; second, publication; third, the free filing tools and automatic population of filings; fourth, the expedited handling of complaints near an election under \u00a7 2-5(b); and fifth, every remaining duty.\n\n**(e) No displacement.** Amounts appropriated under this Act are in addition to, and shall not be derived from, amounts otherwise available to any agency for other purposes. No agency shall reduce, delay, or discontinue any function it performed before enactment in order to carry out this Act.\n\n**(f) Annual certification of adequacy.** Each agency with duties under this Act shall certify annually whether appropriated amounts are sufficient to perform them, and shall state, where they are not, the amount required and the consequence of the shortfall. The certification is published and transmitted to the Congress.\n\n**(g) Continuity during a lapse in appropriations.** The following are activities authorized by law to continue during a lapse in appropriations, and obligations incurred for them are authorized in advance of appropriations for purposes of section 1342 of title 31, United States Code: the receipt of disclosures under \u00a7 2-1, \u00a7 2-1A, \u00a7 2-1B(e), and \u00a7 2-1C(d); the preservation of those disclosures; and their publication under \u00a7 2-1(c). Publication of a disclosure required within 48 hours shall occur within that period notwithstanding a lapse.\n\n**(h) Excepted personnel.** The officers and employees necessary to perform the activities in subsection (g) shall be designated as excepted from furlough during a lapse in appropriations. Each agency shall identify those positions in its shutdown plan, publish the identification, and maintain staffing sufficient to perform the activities without interruption.\n\n**(i) Deadlines during a lapse.** A deadline imposed by this Act on a covered person, a filer, or any other private party is tolled for the duration of a lapse in appropriations affecting the agency to which the filing is due, and for 14 days thereafter. A deadline imposed on an agency is not tolled as to the activities in subsection (g)."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"1. **Serving-official compensation threshold** (\u00a7 1-5(a)): [$___ or grade level]\n2. **Bundler disclosure threshold** (\u00a7 2-1A(a)): [$___ aggregate raised per cycle]\n3. **Whistleblower award percentage** (\u00a7 2-3A(a)): [___% of the amount actually recovered]\n4. **Period for the Office to decide a harassment-exemption application** (\u00a7 2-1A(e-1), (e-2)): [___ days]\n5. **Period for the Office to issue or decline a compliance ruling** (\u00a7 2-4): [___ days after a complete disclosure]\n6. **Period after which a near-election complaint is deemed declined absent published expedited procedures** (\u00a7 2-5(b-1)): [___ days]\n7. **Trailing period for compensation for promotional services** (\u00a7 2-1B(b)(2)): [___ days after the last date on which compensation is provided]\n8. **Paid-promotion de minimis threshold** (\u00a7 2-1B(c)(5)): [$___ aggregate compensation from one payer per calendar year]\n9. **Platform preservation period for paid-partnership records** (\u00a7 2-1B(k-1)(2)): [___ years]\n10. **Period during which an acquired account states the date of its acquisition** (\u00a7 2-1C(d)(2)): [___ days after the acquisition]\n11. **Funded-account threshold** (\u00a7 2-1C(j)(1)): [$___ in funds from one covered political organization and persons under common control with it, over four calendar quarters]\n12. **Periods for an opinion under \u00a7 2-4, an application under \u00a7 2-1A(e), and screening under \u00a7 2-5, and the share exceeded constituting a shortfall** (\u00a7 2-5(e)): [___ days; ___ percent]"}],"asOf":"2026-11-14"}}}};
